    <h1>About Play UNO Free</h1>
    <p class="page-subtitle">The story behind the browser-based UNO game built for everyone from two friends to a twenty-person party.</p>

    <h2>Our Mission</h2>
    <p>We started Play UNO Free with one stubborn belief: playing a card game with the people you like should be effortless. No app store detour, no account wall, no "sign in to continue," no surprise paywall three rounds in. You should be able to send a link, have your friends tap it, and be dealing cards within seconds — whether they are on a phone on the bus, a laptop at work, or a tablet on the couch across the world.</p>
    <p>That principle shapes every decision we make. Playing never requires an account. Nothing about the core game costs money, and it never will. And because it runs entirely in the browser, there is nothing to install and nothing to update — the game is always the latest version the moment you open it.</p>

    <h2>What Makes Us Different</h2>
    <p>Most online UNO games quietly cap you at four players. We built Play UNO Free to seat <strong>up to 20 players in a single room</strong>, which changes what the game can be. Suddenly it works for a whole classroom, a Discord server, an extended family spread across three time zones, or a company team-building call that everyone secretly enjoys. Big tables play differently — we automatically switch to a double deck at 11+ players so hands stay healthy — and we have written a whole <a href="/20-player-uno">guide to hosting large games</a> because they deserve their own strategy.</p>
    <p>We also went deep on how the game <em>feels</em>. The animations are smooth enough to rival a native app, the card interactions are tuned for touch as much as mouse, and the real-time engine is built to survive the messiness of real life. Players drop off Wi-Fi, close laptops, and wander away mid-turn — so the game reconnects you automatically with your hand preserved, and an auto-play system keeps the table moving when someone goes quiet. One distracted player never freezes the game for everyone else.</p>

    <h2>Built for the Way People Actually Play</h2>
    <p>Nobody plays UNO strictly by the rulebook, so we did not build it that way. Alongside the official Classic ruleset, we offer <a href="/uno-no-mercy">No Mercy</a> — stacking, Wild +8s, and elimination at 25 cards — and a fully Custom mode where the host toggles each house rule individually: <a href="/rules/stacking">stacking</a>, Seven-Zero, Jump-In, Draw to Match, Force Play, Wild Challenge, Play for Places, turn timers, and more. Whatever version you grew up arguing about, you can recreate it here. Our <a href="/game-modes">game modes guide</a> breaks down all three.</p>
    <p>We even added a few things a physical deck never had: the exclusive Wild Draw Eight card, spectator mode for watching a game in progress, one-click invite links that drop friends straight into your room, and preset emoji reactions so the table stays lively and family-friendly without an open chat box.</p>

    <h2>Free, and Fair About It</h2>
    <p>Play UNO Free is genuinely free to play, with no gameplay hidden behind payment. There are coins, levels, daily rewards, and challenges — but coins are earned by playing, never bought, and everything they unlock is cosmetic. A card theme or victory effect will never give anyone an advantage at the table. Optional ads help keep the servers running, and signing in (by email or Google) is entirely optional — it simply saves your stats, coins, and cosmetics across devices.</p>

    <h2>Built With Love</h2>
    <p>Play UNO Free is an independent, indie project — not a faceless corporate release. It is built and maintained by a small effort that genuinely cares about the game, and it improves constantly: new features, better performance, and fixes driven directly by player feedback. If something feels off or you have an idea, our <a href="/contact">contact page</a> goes straight to the people who work on it.</p>

    <h2>The Technology</h2>
    <p>For the curious, the game is built on web fundamentals rather than a heavyweight framework, which is part of why it loads fast on modest devices:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Frontend:</strong> HTML5 Canvas rendering with vanilla JavaScript for a fast, dependency-light client</li>
      <li><strong>Backend:</strong> Node.js and Express serving the game and content</li>
      <li><strong>Real-time:</strong> WebSockets (Socket.io) for instant, low-latency multiplayer and seamless reconnection</li>
    </ul>

